TensorFold patch 0002: EXL3 routed experts for prompt pieces (prefill) on a kernel that decodes each weight tile once.

Why: Flash Next's EXL3 prompt path ran the routed experts on the grouped decode kernel in 1024-row windows. That
kernel takes 16 members a program and decodes the expert's trellis again for every 16 (each expert gets ~20 members
a window), launches ~1.3M mostly-empty programs, runs at 25% occupancy and issues on ~16% of cycles; its grouping
runs in one block (2-5 ms a call). 57% of prefill GPU time went to it (profile 2026-10-12): ~650 tok/s uncached.

What:
- cuda/exl3/experts_prefill.cuh (new): prefill_kernel. A program = one pass of 128 members of one expert x 320
  columns x one matrix, 10 warps of 8 member tiles x 2 column tiles (each decoded weight tile feeds 16 mma). Each
  BK=32 stage cp.async-stages the trellis words of its 320 columns (3 stages); input fragments come by ldmatrix.
  Gate/up stage the members' raw bf16 rows a 128-wide K chunk at a time and rotate them in the kernel (prefill_rot:
  rot_in's arithmetic and butterfly order, bit for bit), so rot_in no longer writes an fp16 copy for every slot (~460
  MB a 4096-row piece a layer); down stages Xd. A work item's column strips are neighbouring programs, so they read
  its inputs from L2. Each output's K sum runs in one warp in K order: deterministic, independent of the window and
  of the other members (tests). Z gets one split, so the existing epilogues run with SK = 1. Shared memory is sized
  for the widest trellis a launch takes: the 4-bit experts get their own launch, any other width (Flash Next's 6-bit
  shared expert) a second one.
- experts.cu: group_any (count / scan / scatter, any R) also writes a work list of (expert, pass) items, so the
  shared expert (picked by every row) spreads over many programs. ~0.05 ms instead of 2-5 ms. down_sum: the slots'
  weighted sum without writing each slot's y.
- experts.py: routed(..., prompt=True[, summed=True]) takes that path when the shapes fit (prompt_kernel_fits);
  extension renamed tensorfold_exl3_experts_v2 so cached v1 builds are not reused.
- forward.py: a plain prompt piece's MoE runs in one routed(prompt, summed) call and returns (3, [1, R, D] sum): the
  write-back's mode 3 rounds it to bf16 as mode 2 does (the mixed prompt+decode pass keeps mode 2). The prompt-side
  hyper-connection write-back + norm use the existing fused kernel (hc_fused.write_norm, byte-checked once a device)
  for EXL3's fp16 matrices too, and the EXL3 hyper-connection down uses F16.prefill.
- exl3_mm.py: F16.prefill, one launch of shape-chosen tiles (prompt_tiles, tuned on GB10) over the whole K for a
  prompt piece (it ran as ~16 128-row launches of 16-row tiles): hyper-connection down 1.15 -> 0.54 ms (= cuBLAS).
  upmix_f16: the hyper-connection's up projection and glue.hc_mix in one kernel, the up rows (bf16, 84 MB a 4096-row
  piece) never written or read back: 1.35 -> 0.65 ms; forward.py's _readout_plain uses it for an EXL3 prompt.
- exl3_pack.py: PROMPT_ROWS from TF_EXL3_PROMPT_ROWS (default 2048 as upstream; the recipe sets 4096): the EXL3
  prompt piece size (engine.py, exl3.py's buffers, the admission) and MOE_WINDOW (= the piece, one prompt-kernel call;
  was 1024). A piece reads every routed expert's weights once, so 4096 halves those reads a token.
- tests/cuda/test_exl3_prompt_experts.py: vs the grouped kernel (mixed widths, shared expert), window independence,
  in-kernel rotation bit for bit, the summed path, F16.prefill (vs the chunked tiling, window independence), upmix_f16
  vs up-then-mix.

Numbers (bench at Flash Next's shapes, 2048 rows, 4-bit): gate+up 30.4 -> 5.3 ms, down 14.5 -> 3.6 ms (x5.4),
grouping 5.3 -> 0.03 ms. Outputs: K sums equal to fp32 rounding; after the fp16 epilogue max 2e-3 / norm 1e-4 rel.

Base: TensorFold 609ca419 + the recipe's vision-prefix-cache.patch + local 0001.

